==== Learning IFR

IFR is not a different way to fly — it is the same flying, done when you cannot see, which changes everything about how you think. Wave 2's job is to make the instrument world feel normal: the scan, the procedures, and the calm of an approach flown to minimums in the murk. The Challenger 650 is the classroom, but everything here transfers to every instrument panel you will ever sit behind.

===== The Instrument Scan

The foundation of all instrument flying is the scan: a deliberate, continuous pattern of reading the instruments in a fixed order so that nothing gets ignored when it matters. The order is not arbitrary. *Attitude* is the anchor — the artificial horizon tells you pitch and bank directly, and it is the one instrument that cannot lie to you about what the aircraft is doing. Everything else confirms or refines it: *airspeed* tells you whether the attitude you set is producing the performance you want; *altitude* is the contract with ATC and the terrain; *heading* is where the aircraft points; *vertical speed* is the trend, a moment behind the others, so it confirms rather than leads; and *the needle* — localizer, glideslope, or course — is the path itself.

The classic way to practice is to fly a plain, boring leg in clear weather with the scan running the whole time: pick an altitude, hold it, hold a heading, and keep the scan moving — a few seconds per instrument, never staring at any one. The day it matters — ceiling at minimums, no horizon, an approach to fly — the scan is already a habit, and habits do not need thinking.

===== The Cold & Dark Start

Starting the Challenger from cold and dark is the wave's ritual, and it teaches the aircraft's systems in the most natural way possible: one by one, in the order they need each other. Begin with the cockpit setup — avionics on, the FMS booted, and the flight plan loaded and cross-checked against the LittleNavMap plan you built on the ground; a mismatch is far cheaper to find with the aircraft parked. Then the start flow, per the aircraft's checklist: fuel on, the start sequence, and the temperature limits watched like a hawk — the first start of the day is where hot starts happen. Finish by programming the day into the boxes: performance numbers from the FMS, the departure procedure loaded, the altimeter set. When the aircraft is ready before the passengers are, the start was good.

===== The IFR Leg Flow

Every IFR leg is the same skeleton, and flying the skeleton in the same order every time is what makes the work feel manageable. It starts with the clearance — read it back, load it, fly it: "cleared as filed" or the full route, the SID if there is one, the altitude. The departure then takes you up the procedure as published: altitude and course changes at the fixes, not when you feel like it. In the climb and cruise, ATC (the sim's, in our case) steps you up, the autopilot holds the mode you selected, and you manage the radio and the fuel. The descent is where the flight is actually won: the STAR if there is one, then vectors or the arrival — and the approach is *briefed before you reach it*, not after, because an unbriefed approach is the instrument pilot's version of flying blind. Finally the approach itself: armed, captured, and flown to minimums, with the missed approach already decided. Same skeleton every leg, and the skeleton is the skill.

===== The Approach Briefing

The briefing is the approach done in miniature, on the ground or in the cruise, from the plate in AviTab. Work through it in the same order every time: the *setup* — runway, frequency, the navaid or RNAV waypoints, and the initial approach fix; the *path* — courses, altitudes, and the final approach course, including where the glideslope or lateral capture should happen; the *minimums* — decision altitude and visibility, and what you must see to continue; the *missed approach* — where you climb, what you turn to, and what you fly next; and the *go/no-go* — the weather at the field and the fuel to the alternate. Brief it the same way a hundred times and the hundred-and-first — in real weather, at the end of a long day — briefs itself.

===== Flying the ILS

The ILS is the classic instrument approach, and flying it well is a study in patience. Intercept the localizer — the lateral needle — well before the final approach fix, at the altitude the plate specifies, and let the needle come to you rather than chasing it; an overshoot is almost always the result of trying to catch a needle that was already converging. When the glideslope centers, capture it: pitch for the path, power for the speed, and make small corrections — the instruments reward the pilot who nudges, not the one who grabs. Crosswind complicates it honestly: you crab into the wind to hold the localizer, and the wind correction is part of the approach, not an error.

At the decision altitude the briefing pays off: if the runway environment is in sight, continue to a normal landing; if it is not, the missed approach is not a failure — it is the procedure you briefed two hundred miles ago. Hand-fly the first attempts, even if the autopilot can do it, because the autopilot will not be there the day the needles matter most. The ILS is where the scan becomes a dance; let it.

===== The Missed Approach

The missed approach is part of the approach, not an emergency, and the distinction is what makes it calm. When you call the go-around — at minimums without the runway, or any time the approach is not working — the sequence is fixed: power and pitch, climb thrust and nose to the missed altitude; clean up, with the flaps retracting on schedule as speed builds; then fly the plate, the published missed procedure with its climb, its turn, and its hold or its next approach. And then decide: hold, try again, or go to the alternate — a decision that was already made on the ground, because the fuel and the plan were built around it. Fly the missed approach often enough that it is boring, and it will never be frightening.

===== The Practice Progression

Learn IFR the way you learned the pattern: in layers. First, run the scan on clear-weather legs until it is a reflex — fly a leg at a set altitude and heading and make the instruments your scenery. Second, fly one complete IFR leg a day, clearance to touchdown, all of it, even in perfect weather — the skeleton becomes muscle memory. Third, fly it in weather: real minimums, real decisions, and the quiet satisfaction of an approach that works when it has to. Fourth, shoot the same approach twice — once with the autopilot flying it, once by hand — and compare: the autopilot shows you what the aircraft can do, the hand-fly shows you what you can do. When the whole flow feels like routine, the Q400 in Wave 3 puts it to work at regional pace, where the routine is the schedule.
